;   Aggression predicate — second piece of the shared drawing layer.
;
;   Issue #3 draws health bars for the *aggressive* enemies, so the layer needs
;   one shared answer to "is this object attacking?", and it must read the same
;   fields the game reads, in the same way, or the health bars will name a
;   different set of objects than the game's own combat does.
;
;   Measured where the game itself defines aggression (Beta 1 source):
;
;     * The per-type table obchars (MISC.SRC:1425, at $D90D in this build)
;       names all 44 object types through the `c` macro. Its field comments
;       (MISC.SRC:1405-1424) define exactly the agression axis that issue #3
;       needs: `oc_attak equ 3 ;interact : "he/she ignores you." cnt=0:attack`
;       — and the types that carry oc_attak are the monsters (mushroom,
;       troll, spectre, monk, winged serpent, werebat, venworm, skeleton,
;       spider, stoneman, rat, fire elemental, demon, abadon, statue), while
;       people (boatman, beggar, wise man, ...) carry oc_noone/oc_ignor/
;       oc_inter and things carry oc_noone.
;     * At activation the game copies that byte into the live field: actob
;       (X6.PDS:516-549) does `lda obchars,y : sta obchr,x` (X6.PDS:522-523),
;       Y=type. So obchr IS obchars[type], made mutable — the game then edits
;       it per instance (e.g. `eor #%00001100` "set to always ignore",
;       X4.PDS:373-374; bullets explode/damage, X2.PDS:269-271).
;     * obtyp is X0.PDS:542's `zp obtyp,maxob ;type/active flag`. Bit 7 SET
;       means the object is deleted/unused: X1.PDS:45-46 wipes the table with
;       `sec / ror obtyp,x` (carry in = bit 7 set), and every active test in
;       the tree reads it the same way — `ldy obtyp,x : bpl` at X3.PDS:182,
;       X4.PDS:52, X4.PDS:314, X6.PDS:516.
;     * obchr is X0.PDS:552's `zp obchr,maxob ;characteristics`, laid out by
;       the `c` macro at X0.PDS:165-168: `@1` physical/ethereal in bit 7, `@2`
;       searchability in bit 6, `@3` physical bullet action in bits 5-4, `@4`
;       attack/interact CONTROL in bits 3-2, `@5` the inter/attack COUNT in
;       bits 1-0.
;     * The interaction handler consumes those two fields together
;       (X4.PDS:353-357): `and #$03` reads the count, then
;       `lsr / lsr / and #$03` reads the control, and pages through exactly
;       four cases — X4.PDS:359 "0=always no one there", 1 "always he/she
;       ignores you", 2 "interact if cnt=0 else he/she ignores you", 3
;       (X4.PDS:403) "attack if cnt=0 else he/she ignores you", where 3+cnt=0
;       sets the object's event flag to PERMANENT ATTACK (X4.PDS:404).
;
;   So an object is aggressive exactly when it is active AND its obchr control
;   is 3 AND its count has run out. That is this router's own branch, copied
;   from the game's, not a re-derivation.
;
;   obmod is measured and deliberately NOT in the predicate: X4.PDS:101 routes
;   by mode value into movement/animation handlers (`dw jumping,falling,...` at
;   X4.PDS:247-248), and none of the m_* equates (X4.PDS:234-244) says
;   anything about hostility — a m_walk skeleton is as hostile as a m_walk
;   barbarian, per obchr, while the per-object m_* handlers get their drive
;   from event flags and animation timings. Journal 18's open question
;   "obmod vs obstat" is answered the same way: neither carries it; obchr
;   does.
;
;   IN:  X = object index, 0..maxob-1 (caller's job to keep in range)
;   OUT: A = 1 if the object is aggressive toward the player right now,
;        0 if not. X and Y preserved. N/V/C reflect A as per `lda #`.
;   USES: A only
;
;   Written by us. Eurocom's source is read-only and pinned; see README.md.

        org $fb0a
obaggro  lda obtyp,x
        bmi obaggro_no
        lda obchr,x
        and #$0c
        cmp #$0c
        bne obaggro_no
        lda obchr,x
        and #$03
        bne obaggro_no
        lda #$01
        rts
obaggro_no
        lda #$00
        rts
